Z||000000||drednot||08-22-2001||12:56 PM||||What do I lubericate the clutch release bearing with?  what else should I lube while I am at it?||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||08-22-2001||01:24 PM||||You are talking about the collar on the trans that it slides in and out on.  I use graphite anti sieze on it.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forsalestart.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Archie||08-22-2001||01:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by PBJ:<BR><B>You are talking about the collar on the trans that it slides in and out on.  I use graphite anti sieze on it.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And use very little of it & use on the inside diameter only.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||08-22-2001||01:57 PM||||also the clutch fork pivot shaft. Ive seen them corrode inside and make the pedal very stiff. Might squirt some WD40 in it overnite then grease it with waterproof lithium grease||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||08-22-2001||07:29 PM||||Yes, use only a TINY bit of anti-sieze.  Tiny.  like almost nothing.  just barely enough to prevent corrosion.<P>You do NOT want any of this to ever end up on your pressure plate!!!<BR>(it will slip, then get hot, then glaze, then Explode* then fail.)<P>*= If you're Tina :) (though I don't think her clutch failure had anything to do with excess lubrication :) I Pitty her new Centreforce, GO TINA!!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
